    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIf we wish to sum the squares of the first [MATH: N] integers, an easy way is to use the following formula:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n^{2}|= 1^{2} + 2^{2} + 3^{2} + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1)^{2} + N^{2}; = 1 + 4 + 9 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1)^{2} + N^{2}; = (1)/(6)N(N + 1)(2N + 1)]\n\nContent:\nSince we also know that\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n|= 1 + 2 + 3 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1) + N; = (1)/(2)N(N + 1),]\n\nContent:\nwe can use these formulas to evaluate any series of the form\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N (an^2+bn+c)}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of the First N Square Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the formula\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n^2 = \\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)]\n\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n^{2}|= (1)/(6) \u22c5 100(100 + 1)(2 \u22c5 100 + 1); = (1)/(6) \u22c5 100 \u22c5 101 \u22c5 201; = 338350.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 42952]\nb. [MATH: 42725]\nc. [MATH: 42625]\nd. [MATH: 42925]\ne. [MATH: 42825]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(30)n^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 9575]\nb. [MATH: 9395]\nc. [MATH: 9455]\nd. [MATH: 9515]\ne. [MATH: 9635]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Finite Quadratic Series with a Constant Factor\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\n\\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we take the factor [MATH: 4] outside the summation:\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^2 = 4\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^2]\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n^2 = \\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)]\n\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: 4\u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n^{2}|= 4((1)/(6) \u22c5 100(100 + 1)(2 \u22c5 100 + 1)); = 4 \u22c5 (1)/(6) \u22c5 100 \u22c5 101 \u22c5 201; = 4 \u22c5 338350; = 1353400.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(60)(n^{2})/(11).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6710]\nb. [MATH: 8540.4]\nc. [MATH: 6930.6]\nd. [MATH: 41820]\ne. [MATH: 40260]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(100)12n^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3060200]\nb. [MATH: 5060200]\nc. [MATH: 4060200]\nd. [MATH: 6060200]\ne. [MATH: 2060200]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Quadratic Series with a Linear or Constant Term\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\n\\sum_{n=1}^{50} (3n^2+2n).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nNotice that we can write the given summation as follows:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{2} + 2n)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)3n^{2} + \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)2n; = 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2} + 2\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n]\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two series using our formulas.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n[MATH: \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n^2 = \\dfrac1 6 N (N+1)(2N+1)]\nwith [MATH: N=50,] and we get\n[MATH: 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n^{2}|= 3((1)/(6) \u22c5 50(50 + 1)(2 \u22c5 50 + 1)); = 3 \u22c5 (1)/(6) \u22c5 50 \u22c5 51 \u22c5 101; = 3 \u22c5 42925; = 128775.]\n- For the second series, we use the formula\n[MATH: \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac1 2 N (N+1)]\nwith [MATH: N=50,] and we get\n[MATH: 2\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n|= 2((1)/(2) \u22c5 50 \u22c5 (50 + 1)); = 2 \u22c5 1275; = 2550.]\n\nFinally, we have\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{2} + 2n)|= 128775 + 2550; = 131325.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)(6n^{2} + 3n + 2)] in terms of [MATH: N.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (N)/(2)[4N^{2} + 13N + 5]]\nb. [MATH: (N)/(6)[2N^{2} + 17N + 5]]\nc. [MATH: (N)/(2)[2N^{2} + 13N + 5]]\nd. [MATH: (N)/(2)[4N^{2} + 9N + 9]]\ne. [MATH: (N)/(2)[2N^{2} + 9N + 9]]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)(2n^{2} + 3n).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 79675]\nb. [MATH: 89475]\nc. [MATH: 89675]\nd. [MATH: 83435]\ne. [MATH: 87675]"
